On conversations with Tyler, economist Darren Assimigou examines what happens when values conflict and utility or cost-benefit analysis offers no mechanical answer.

62
00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:09.720
It matters wherever technology policy pits efficiency against rights, power, and protections that only institutions can provide.

63
00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:38.600
So if if we're siguic, we might say, well, when values clash, we look to utility.

64
00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:42.520
Or maybe it'd be cost-benefit analysis to decide which gets priority.

65
00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:43.960
And you're not willing to say that.

66
00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:44.520
So why not?

67
00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:45.880
What's wrong with that answer?

68
00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:50.360
Because I think So let me start with what's wrong with a general will type answer.

69
00:06:50.520 --> 00:07:01.320
I'm simplifying it, but I would say in a general will type of approach is that we have a book in our hand which says when values clash and when there's something, here is a higher value, and we're gonna follow that.

70
00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:06.760
And ultimately, even though he's a true liberal, John Rawls also goes into that direction.

71
00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:12.360
Now, you might think utilitarianism avoids that, because it says, no, we don't have a book.

72
00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:16.000
All we do is we turn everything to utils and then we compare those utils.

73
00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:20.080
But who decides how to compare how to turn things into utils?

74
00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:24.080
Who decides, you know, when there are externalities that are important?

75
00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:26.160
You know, there again needs to be a book.

76
00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:31.200
And I don't like that book, because who decides that what that book is, and how can we be certain that that book exists?

77
00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:44.000
So that's why once we have the building blocks of individual liberty, then I think we have to go back to society and seek consensus on what our temporary book should be.

78
00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:48.320
There's a lot of talk in your book about what you call working class liberalism.

79
00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:51.920
But say today in America, what exactly is working class?

80
00:07:51.920 --> 00:07:55.680
You know, household income, it's almost six figures.

81
00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:58.960
Percentage working in manufacturing, it's what, eight to nine percent.

82
00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:01.200
Private sector unions are very small.

83
00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:04.240
Is this even a meaningful notion today, the working class?

84
00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:06.000
May or may not be.

85
00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:31.600
That's why I say that social democracy as it evolved in Scandinavia and then spread to Europe and to some extent in a uh modified version to the United States, isn't the right actualization of liberal ideas today, because it very strongly relies on unions and a particular kind of industrial working class.

86
00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:33.440
Today we don't have an industrial working class.

87
00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:39.200
So if you're by working class, I meant industrial working class, obviously that would be almost a vacuous notion.

88
00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:47.440
But we have the vast majority of people who live by earning their living in the labor market.

89
00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:48.880
That's the working class.

90
00:08:48.880 --> 00:09:01.480
If you're getting your income from the labor market mostly, not from capital, not from your parents, then in my definition, you're part of the working class.

91
00:09:01.480 --> 00:09:06.920
And the working class is distinguished by having a very, very diverse set of skills.

92
00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:08.120
Not just one skill.

93
00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:16.200
It's going to have the manual part, it's going to have the trades, it's going to have office jobs, it's going to have entrepreneurship of some sort.

94
00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:18.840
But that's what the unifying theme is.

95
00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:21.240
And that's, of course, not a very simple unifying theme.

96
00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:23.480
So there's going to be a lot of heterogeneity within the working class.

97
00:09:23.480 --> 00:09:29.880
That's why I am emphasize in the working class liberalism, different communities that are going to have their own practices and values.

98
00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:34.840
Some choices aren't optimization problems.

99
00:09:34.840 --> 00:09:39.320
They're judgments about which protections a society refuses to trade away.

